From 8a7abc333b053aeb6006de357c68063606fa62cc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Dawid=20Wr=C3=B3bel?= <me@dawidwrobel.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 10:07:40 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 12/41] tee: qseecom: drop the references the core took before
 replacing a parameter

TEE_IOC_SUPPL_RECV resolves any memref the caller passed and takes a reference
for it, and the core deliberately does not release them afterwards -- see the
comment on free_params() in tee_ioctl_supp_recv(), which explains that
supp_recv may consume and replace its parameters, so the usual cleanup cannot
run.

That makes the references the driver's to drop, and this driver overwrote
param[0] wholesale without doing so. A tee_shm pins the context that allocated
it, so each leaked reference strands a context, tee_device_unregister() then
waits for it for ever, and the module is left in "going" state that only a
reboot clears -- the same failure this driver already documents for a different
cause. Any client able to open the privileged device triggers it just by
passing a memref.

Drop them, and refuse anything that is not an empty parameter rather than
silently discarding it, as OP-TEE does.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Assisted-by: Kimi:kimi-k3
---
 drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c | 21 +++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c b/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c
index a0ebf5a27..125edea74 100644
--- a/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c
+++ b/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c
@@ -970,11 +970,32 @@ static int qseecom_tee_supp_recv(struct tee_context *ctx, u32 *func,
 	struct qseecom_tee *qtee = ctxdata->qtee;
 	struct qseecom_tee_supp *supp = &qtee->supp;
 	struct qseecom_tee_listener *listener;
+	unsigned int i;
 	int ret;
 
 	if (*num_params < 1)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
+	/*
+	 * The core resolved any memref the caller passed and took a reference
+	 * for it, and deliberately does not drop them on this path -- see the
+	 * comment on free_params() in tee_ioctl_supp_recv(). Since param[0] is
+	 * about to be overwritten wholesale, dropping them is ours to do, and
+	 * failing to leaves a reference on a tee_shm, which pins the context,
+	 * which strands the device on unregister.
+	 *
+	 * Nothing meaningful can be passed in, so refuse anything that is not
+	 * empty rather than silently discarding it.
+	 */
+	for (i = 0; i < *num_params; i++) {
+		if (tee_param_is_memref(param + i) && param[i].u.memref.shm)
+			tee_shm_put(param[i].u.memref.shm);
+
+		if ((param[i].attr & TEE_IOCTL_PARAM_ATTR_TYPE_MASK) !=
+		    TEE_IOCTL_PARAM_ATTR_TYPE_NONE)
+			return -EINVAL;
+	}
+
 	ret = wait_event_interruptible(supp->wq,
 				       qseecom_tee_supp_pending(supp));
 	if (ret)
